# Airport CEO Brief — Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals

**Author:** Airport CEO (chair-facing lens)
**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals (IAD + DCA)
**Date:** 2026-08-06

The Strategist will read this as one voice among many. My job is to say what the thesis costs and what it buys in the only currency a large-hub CEO can spend: bond covenants, board votes, airline signatures, and 30-year amortization schedules. I am not going to argue the acoustics. I am going to argue whether MWAA's institutional position lets us build the acoustics we want.

## 1. Key findings

- **The window is closing, not opening.** MWAA signed a new 15-year Use and Lease on January 1, 2025 tied to a $9.0B baseline CIP, and on July 2026 the White House, DOT, United, and MWAA announced a $20–22.5B expansion of the Dulles program. Concourse E opens autumn 2026. A quiet-by-design standard adopted before the expansion program's design manuals harden is a rounding error on $20B. Adopted after, it is a change order and an MII fight. The decision is a calendar decision more than an engineering decision.
- **This thesis is credit-neutral if we run it right, credit-negative if we let it slip the Concourse E opening.** Moody's Aa3 / S&P AA- / Fitch AA- ratings sit on debt service coverage, days-cash-on-hand, and single-carrier concentration — none of which a design standard touches directly. What ratings agencies punish is schedule slippage on a signature opening and the appearance of governance drift. The risk is not the standard; the risk is missing the fall 2026 Concourse E ribbon-cutting because we bolted acoustics onto a design that is already at IFC. [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-bond-ratings]
- **The MII is the real gatekeeper, not the board.** MWAA's 2025 U&L is a hybrid-residual with an Extraordinary Coverage Protection at 125% and a settlement mechanism. Any material scope addition inside the $20–22.5B envelope will trigger MII review against United (IAD dominant) and American (DCA dominant). United's leverage today is at a generational peak: it holds a fresh long-term Dulles lease and co-announced the expansion with the White House. Getting United's engineering standards group to co-author the quiet standard is worth more than any board resolution.
- **PFC is frozen at $4.50 and the federal stack will not pay for "quiet."** The FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 did not raise the PFC cap and holds AIP at $4B/year through FY2028. There is no BIL/IIJA line item for terminal acoustics. Every dollar of incremental cost from this standard flows to the airline rate base through the residual, or it comes out of MWAA's own reserves. That is a rate-and-charges conversation with two hub carriers, not a grant application. [Source: https://aaae.org/uscta-alerts/lawmakers-reach-agreement-on-final-faa-bill]
- **DCA now sits inside a safety-politics envelope that changes the shape of any PA policy.** The January 29, 2025 midair collision, the seven-week fall 2025 federal shutdown, and the 5.3% DCA passenger decline together mean that any recommendation to reduce announcements at DCA will be read by the Virginia and Maryland delegations, and by NTSB, through a safety lens. The standard must be defensible as *more* legible communication under irregular ops, not less.
- **A 17-member, four-appointer board can survive a design standard; it cannot survive a policy fight over "silence."** MWAA's board is appointed by the Governor of Virginia (7), the Mayor of DC (4), the President (3), and the Governor of Maryland (3), with staggered six-year terms. Presidential appointees will churn with administrations. Anything that depends on a stable political coalition for enforcement is fragile; anything embedded in the Design Manual and the U&L Design Standards attachment is durable across chair transitions.
- **Long-tenure pattern: the standards that survived were the ones embedded before the money was authorized.** SFO's early ADA integration, DFW's Skylink alignment with the original terminal geometry, and DEN's failure to embed baggage-system requirements in the original construction bid are the three most instructive precedents. Each of them is a story about whether a standard entered the room before the design contract was signed or after.

## 2. Evidence

### 2.1 The bond-rating lens

MWAA carries Moody's Aa3 / S&P AA- / Fitch AA- on airport-system revenue debt, all stable, with roughly $4.9B outstanding across 19 series as of mid-2025. A June 2025 issuance placed a 2036 AMT maturity at ~4.37%. Between 2025 and 2028 MWAA plans roughly $5.5B of new debt, projected to push adjusted debt-per-O&D-enplanement to ~$400 by 2028 and CPE to $20.71 by 2030 in the Series 2025A Official Statement's Report of the Airport Consultant. [Source: https://www.bondbuyer.com/news/mwaa-coming-to-market-with-829-million-municipal-bond-deal] [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/dulles-rfi-analysis]

Rating agencies score airports on debt service coverage, days-cash-on-hand, cost-per-enplanement trajectory, liquidity, and — the factor most relevant to us — carrier concentration. Moody's scorecard methodology carries a "Carrier Base Quality" subfactor keyed to the credit of the dominant carrier and the concentration percentage. Concentration above ~50% is a rating headwind; below ~40% it is a tailwind. IAD's United hub position and DCA's American hub position both sit inside the concentration band where the identity of the dominant carrier's own credit rating meaningfully moves the airport's scorecard. [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-bond-ratings]

The comparative benchmark is San Francisco: S&P upgraded SFO to AA- in January 2025 with 245 days cash on hand, 98%+ fixed-rate debt, and $1.2B in uncommitted reserves. That is the shape of a large-hub credit that has room to absorb a discretionary standards program. A CEO who commits MWAA to a new capital envelope without a comparable liquidity cushion is asking the ratings agencies to score MWAA on a different curve. [Source: https://spglobal.com/research/articles/231107-u-s-transportation-infrastructure-airport-update-the-industry-is-cleared-for-takeoff-with-cost-pressures-on-12903444]

The material rating question this thesis raises is not "does quiet-by-design cost too much." It is: does the standard's design-review discipline slip the Concourse E opening or the expansion program's phased delivery? A single-quarter delay on a signature airside opening does more to the next issuance's spread than any acoustic scope will.

### 2.2 The airline use-and-lease structure

MWAA's new 15-year U&L took effect January 1, 2025 and runs through December 31, 2039, replacing the prior residual-style agreement. Public disclosure describes it as a hybrid-residual with an Extraordinary Coverage Protection provision that triggers when projected airport revenues plus Transfers, less O&M, fall below 125% of that airport's senior and subordinated bond debt service, with a 120-day settlement mechanism. Revenue-sharing runs 55/45 at DCA and through a Plateau mechanism at IAD. [Source: https://www.mwaa.com/news/metropolitan-washington-airports-authority-completes-negotiations-new-use-and-lease-agreement] [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airline-use-agreements]

The MII terms — the exact thresholds, project categories, and airline signatory list — are not verified inside this evidence pass. The context packet explicitly names this as a gap not to be filled by industry-convention inference. What is documented is the political shape: the U&L was adopted alongside the $9.0B baseline CIP, and the July 2026 announcement raises the IAD program to $20–22.5B. A quiet-by-design standard adopted before the expansion program's Design Manual amendment is a cost embedded in the base project cost of Concourses C/D replacement and the Concourse E follow-on tranches. Adopted after, it is either a rate-and-charges add or a change order — either of which walks into MII territory.

United's leverage right now is unusually concentrated. It executed a separate long-term Dulles lease in the 2024–2025 cycle, it holds the dominant IAD gate position, and it co-announced the expansion with the White House and DOT Secretary Duffy. American, at DCA, is inside a safety-politics envelope from the January 2025 collision and holds the dominant DCA position. The right question is not "will the airlines sign off"; it is "which carrier's engineering standards group is the standard's co-author, and which one gets to say yes to it last." From the chair, that answer is United on the IAD side and American on the DCA side, and the standard should be drafted with that sequencing in mind.

### 2.3 The federal stack

PFC remains capped at $4.50 per segment ($18 round-trip maximum). The FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 (Pub. L. 118-63) did not raise the cap; it added streamlining language for PFC applications. AIP is authorized at $4B/year through FY2028. BIL/IIJA's airport terminal program is a supplemental competitive line, not a discretionary funding source we can rely on. [Source: https://aaae.org/uscta-alerts/lawmakers-reach-agreement-on-final-faa-bill] [Source: https://www.faa.gov/airports/pfc]

Two federal partners set the ceiling on what "quiet" can mean inside our own building. CBP governs FIS-hall communication at IAD; MWAA cannot unilaterally reduce announcements inside the sterile international arrivals process. TSA sets the security-announcement rulebook and has been trending toward operator flexibility on cadence and wording, but the current TSA guidance was not verified in this pass and should not be assumed. Any standard that pretends to govern PA volume in CBP and TSA space is a standard that will not survive a federal-partner review.

The FAA next reauthorizes in 2028. There is no federal window between now and then during which MWAA can pull a new dedicated funding stream to pay for terminal-acoustic upgrades. That fixes the funding architecture: general revenue, incremental debt, and airline rates. Nothing else.

### 2.4 Board governance

MWAA's 17-member board is appointed by four bodies: 7 by the Governor of Virginia (confirmed by the Virginia General Assembly), 4 by the DC Mayor (confirmed by the DC Council), 3 by the President (with Senate advice and consent), and 3 by the Governor of Maryland. Six-year terms, one reappointment. The 2026 chair is Mark Uncapher; vice chair Alex Vogel; secretary Felice C. Smith. This is not a city department board; MWAA is an independent, interstate public-body corporate under 49 U.S.C. §§ 49101–49112, operating federal property under an 80-year DOT lease.

Two governance realities matter for this thesis. First, Presidential appointees will change with administrations and Senate cycles, so the standard's political survivability depends on being written into instruments that do not depend on any one appointer bloc — the Design Manual, the U&L Design Standards attachment, and the Board Order that adopts the manual amendment. Second, staggered six-year terms mean the standard should be capable of being enforced by a chair three chairs from now, without a briefing on the original intent. That argues for measurable, verifiable requirements (STI, ambient sound, reverberation time, signal-to-noise ratio at named zone boundaries) and against aspirational language.

### 2.5 The host region

MWAA sits at the intersection of Virginia state politics, DC city politics, Maryland state interest, the federal executive branch, and the Virginia and Maryland Congressional delegations. Post-collision, DCA has a heightened political sensitivity that will not fade before the NTSB final report and probably not before the 2028 FAA reauthorization cycle. Any recommendation to modify DCA public-address governance will be read against that backdrop, whether or not the substantive change is safety-neutral.

The natural allies are Fairfax and Loudoun County economic-development officials (IAD growth is their story), the Northern Virginia business community, and — critically — United's IAD operations leadership, which has a documented interest in a differentiated hub product. The natural opposition is any constituency that reads "fewer announcements" as "less safety" — the pilots' unions, the flight-attendant unions, and disability-rights advocacy organizations if the visual and staffed redundancy is not credible on day one. The standard's political success depends on being introduced with the accessibility redundancy already visible in the design, not promised in a later phase.

### 2.6 The CEO's calendar as evidence

I do not run the AOC. Large-hub CEO time in the 2023–2025 cycle has documented itself as political/government-affairs, capital program stewardship, board management, and airline relations — the operational share is small and delegated. Peer CEOs have been spending 2025–2026 on FAA-reauthorization implementation, U&L renegotiations at hubs where the agreement window is open (DFW, ATL, LAX all inside their own transition windows), and — universally — on the post-2020 capital program bulge. Sean Donohue's retirement announcement at DFW after twelve years in-seat is one data point in a broader pattern: the CEO who carries a capital program through opening usually leaves within eighteen months of the ribbon. [Source: https://fortworthreport.org/2024/10/08/dfw-airport-ceo-announces-retirement-in-2025/]

For me, a quiet-by-design standard is a two-hour agenda item at a board work session and roughly a dozen half-hour meetings with United, American, and the design-manual owner. It is not a major CEO time commitment. What *would* be a major time commitment — and this is the case against — is a standard that becomes controversial with a signatory carrier or a federal partner and reaches the board through the airline complaint route rather than the manual amendment route.

### 2.7 Long-tenure strategic patterns

Three quarter-century patterns are load-bearing. First, the standards that survived — SFO's ADA integration in the 2000s, ATL's early biometric-ready infrastructure at Concourse F, LAX's Terminal 6 acoustic and daylighting envelope — all entered the room before the design contract was executed. The ones that failed — DEN's baggage system, the various post-hoc "sensory-friendly" retrofits — arrived after IFC. Second, the CEOs who lost their political position generally did so on a use-and-lease renegotiation or on a capital-program cost overrun, rarely on an operating metric. Third, the winners generally spent political capital when the airline coalition was fragmented and saved it when the coalition was consolidated. Right now, at IAD, the airline coalition is consolidated behind United and the White House. That is not the window in which a CEO expends unilateral political capital. It is the window in which a CEO co-authors.

## 3. The financial-and-political case for and against

### For

- The marginal cost of embedding the standard in the $20–22.5B expansion program is small — realistically low single-digit millions across Concourses C/D replacement and the Concourse E follow-on tranches — versus the retrofit cost of installing directional loudspeakers, PA zoning, and acoustic treatment in occupied terminal space, which industry experience places at 5–10x greenfield cost.
- The standard is credit-neutral to slightly positive: rating agencies read written design standards, commissioning discipline, and post-opening verification as governance strength, not weakness.
- It gives MWAA a defensible answer to accessibility and Section 504 challenges that would otherwise arrive after opening. Litigation risk is a slow-moving but real credit factor.
- It positions MWAA to differentiate the IAD product against BWI and DCA in a post-perimeter, post-slot-exemption competitive frame, at a moment when the 10 new beyond-perimeter DCA slots have shifted origin-and-destination market share.

### Against

- Any standard that raises design cost per gate at IAD without MII pre-clearance is a rate-and-charges conversation with United at the exact moment United's leverage is at its peak. A CEO who loses that conversation loses more than an acoustic standard.
- The standard could be read by the Virginia and Maryland delegations, post-collision, as reducing communication redundancy at DCA. That reading is politically expensive to correct.
- Concourse E is at IFC. Standards that force late-cycle design changes to Concourse E risk slipping the autumn 2026 opening. A ribbon-cutting slip is the single most credit-negative outcome available.
- The Design Manual amendment cycle historically runs 9–18 months. The expansion program will not wait for a manual amendment. The standard has to be adoptable inside the existing manual as a supplement, or it will miss the design-freeze on the expansion program's first tranche.

## 4. Specific data points and precedents a strategist can use

1. **$4.9B outstanding airport-system revenue debt across 19 series; ~$5.5B of new debt planned 2025–2028; CPE forecast $20.71 by 2030; adjusted debt-per-O&D-enplanement peaking ~$400 in 2028.** These are the numbers that frame any incremental cost claim. [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/dulles-rfi-analysis]
2. **PFC frozen at $4.50 since 2000; FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 did not raise the cap.** Any thesis that assumes a federal funding stream for terminal-acoustic upgrades is arithmetically wrong. [Source: https://aaae.org/uscta-alerts/lawmakers-reach-agreement-on-final-faa-bill]
3. **Concourse E: ~435,000 sq ft, 14 United gates, autumn 2026 opening.** The most immediate real-world test of any MWAA quiet-by-design decision. What we specify here becomes the reference standard for Concourses C/D replacement inside the expansion program.
4. **Moody's Carrier Base Quality subfactor.** Concentration above ~50% is a rating headwind; below ~40% is a tailwind. United's IAD position and American's DCA position each sit inside the band where the airline's own credit rating meaningfully moves the airport's scorecard. [Source: https://dwuconsulting.com/dwu-ai/airport-bond-ratings]
5. **Schiphol precedent: passenger-satisfaction score of 3.84/5 attributed in part to the digital-first, announcement-reduced model.** The measurable outcome is real, but Schiphol operates under a single-operator European framework without a signatory-carrier U&L or FAA Part 139 layered regime. Regulatory transfer is not automatic. Any thesis that leans on Schiphol without acknowledging that gap is one an executive reviewer will unwind. [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/schiphol-in-2025-quieter-aircraft-more-satisfied-travellers-and-solid-financial-results/]

## Recommendation from the chair

Adopt the quiet-by-design standard as a Design Manual supplement, co-authored with United's IAD engineering standards group and American's DCA facilities group, before the expansion program's first-tranche IFC. Draft the requirements in verifiable technical terms (STI, ambient sound, reverberation time, signal-to-noise ratio, zone-boundary spillover) so a chair three chairs from now can enforce them without a briefing on the original intent. Do not tie adoption to Concourse E's opening date; treat Concourse E as the reference project against which the standard is subsequently benchmarked, not as the vehicle that carries the standard's first application. And do not make it a policy fight over "silence." Make it a design-discipline fight over intelligibility, redundancy, and lifecycle cost — a fight this CEO knows how to win at the board.
